  "subject": "☎️ Axios Finish Line: Phone a friend",
  "date": "Tue, 01 Sep 2026 21:29:41 -0400",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 🇸🇮 Plus: Slovenian sunrise | Tuesday, September 01, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n PRESENTED BY PEPSICO \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Sep 01, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n Hello, Tuesday! Smart Brevity™ count: 396 words … 1½ mins. Edited by Amy Stern. \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: Close your social rings \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n We track so many elements of our well-being: steps, workouts, sleep. Maybe our friendships deserve a little attention too, Axios' Natalie Daher writes. \n\n 🫶 Why it matters: We know social ties are good for our health. But unlike exercise or hydration, maintaining our relationships rarely makes it onto the daily wellness checklist. The New York Times' Jancee Dunn recently posed the idea of \"social rings\" akin to an Apple Watch's movement-based rings, which users are challenged to \" close \" every day. \n\n Have you connected with a friend lately? If not, maybe your social rings are still open, she says. 👯 Jaimie Arona Krems, director of UCLA's Center for Friendship Research , told Dunn she's surprised friendship isn't treated as more of a basic health recommendation, alongside eating well, drinking water and getting enough sleep. \n\n Flashback: We brought you a similar idea earlier this year with the rise of \"social prescribing.\" The science: Research has linked friendships with better heart health, greater resilience to stress and a stronger immune system, Dunn notes. \n\n ⏰ Even if we understand the benefits, the challenge is often finding time. Dunn offers some suggestions that don't require adding another dinner or weekend plan to an already packed calendar: \n\n 📞 Find your window. Learn when a friend tends to have a free pocket — during a commute, dog walk or dinner prep — and use it for a quick call. We'd all love more intimate, in-person time with our dearest people. But when that's hard to come by, a quick connection still counts. 🧺 Combine friendship with chores. Go to the grocery store together, or put a friend on speaker while you fold laundry or tackle your Sunday cleaning. 💌 Bring back the chatty email. Maybe you'd appreciate the modern-day pen pal. Drop a line! ✍️ Remember the follow-up. If a friend tells you about a doctor's appointment, big interview or family problem, check back in afterward. The bottom line: Friendship can feel like something that should happen naturally. But as life gets busier, a little intentionality becomes essential.\n\n Read the NYT story ( gift link). \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM PEPSICO\r\n\n \r\n How AI helps farmers decide faster \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Farmers often make decisions in real time, under pressure and with narrow windows to protect yield.\n\n\r\n The solution: PepsiCo says AI tools are helping growers spot pest and disease pressure earlier, while also reducing chemical use.\n\n\r\n Read the Q&amp;A. \n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 🌄 Parting shot: Alpine glow \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Photo: Ashley Mahoney A sunrise lights up Lake Bled and its island church in Slovenia, seen from Ojstrica.\n\n Axios Charlotte reporter Ashley Mahoney and her husband hiked up the mountain during their two-week honeymoon in Slovenia, the Dolomites and Venice. \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
