td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0;border-bottom:10px solid #f5f5f5\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>      <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 10px;line-height:1.5\">\r\n<span class=\"b k\" style=\"font-size:27px;font-family:'gorditamedium',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px;line-height:1.5\">  1 big thing: Close your social rings </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>   <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td> <img src=\"https://images.axios.com/0120msjhchavxO_Y2agtlRN9AUk=/0x0:1280x720/2026/08/31/1788215220295.gif\" width=\"600\" style=\"width:100%\" id=\"item02\" alt=\"Animated illustration of an Apple Watch circles graphic. The red green and blue circles move toward completion, but before they hit the end, the red and green circles grow hands, which high five.\"> </td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.3\">\r\n<span class=\"b\" style=\"font-size:13px;color:#848484\"> <p>Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios</p> </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"15\" style=\"height:15px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr> <tr>\r\n<td class=\"v\" style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.75;font-size:17px;font-family:Georgia,serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px\"><p><strong>We track so many</strong> <strong>elements</strong> of our well-being: steps, workouts, sleep. Maybe our friendships deserve a little attention too, <em>Axios' Natalie Daher writes.</em></p><ul><li>🫶 <strong>Why it matters:</strong> We know social ties are good for our health. But unlike exercise or hydration, maintaining our relationships rarely makes it onto the daily wellness checklist.</li></ul><p><strong>The New York Times' Jancee Dunn</strong> recently <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47304098.444010/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cubnl0aW1lcy5jb20vMjAyNi8wOC8yOC93ZWxsL2ZyaWVuZHNoaXAtc29jaWFsLXJpbmdzLmh0bWw_dW5sb2NrZWRfYXJ0aWNsZV9jb2RlPTEuOWxBLlY1cUEuMFJhR0NsVkxVcVphJnNtaWQ9dXJsLXNoYXJlJnV0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aBf8d67b49\" target=\"_blank\">posed</a> the idea of \"social rings\" akin to an Apple Watch's movement-based rings, which users are challenged to \"<a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47304098.444010/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYXBwbGUuY29tL3dhdGNoL2Nsb3NlLXlvdXItcmluZ3MvP3V0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB2b5d1faf\" target=\"_blank\">close</a>\" every day. </p><ul><li><strong>Have you connected</strong> with a friend lately? If not, maybe your social rings are still open, she says.</li></ul><p>👯 <strong>Jaimie Arona Krems,</strong> director of UCLA's Center for <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47304098.444010/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cucHN5Y2gudWNsYS5lZHUvZmFjdWx0eS1wYWdlL2prcmVtcy8_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aBc24b1548\" target=\"_blank\">Friendship Research</a>, told Dunn she's surprised friendship isn't treated as more of a basic health recommendation, alongside eating well, drinking water and getting enough sleep. </p><ul><li><strong>Flashback:</strong> We brought you a similar idea <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47304098.444010/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYXhpb3MuY29tLzIwMjYvMDQvMTYvaGVhbHRoLWNhcmUtc29jaWFsLXByZXNjcmliaW5nLWNvbm5lY3Rpb24_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB68c4a124\" target=\"_blank\">earlier this year</a> with the rise of \"social prescribing.\"</li></ul><p><strong>The science:</strong> Research has linked friendships with better heart health, greater resilience to stress and a stronger immune system, Dunn notes. </p><p>⏰ <strong>Even if we understand</strong> the benefits, the challenge is often finding time. Dunn offers some suggestions that don't require adding another dinner or weekend plan to an already packed calendar: </p><ul><li>📞 <strong>Find your window.</strong> Learn when a friend tends to have a free pocket — during a commute, dog walk or dinner prep — and use it for a quick call. We'd all love more intimate, in-person time with our dearest people. But when that's hard to come by, a quick connection still counts.</li><li>🧺 <strong>Combine friendship with chores.</strong> Go to the grocery store <em>together, </em>or put a friend on speaker while you fold laundry or tackle your Sunday cleaning.</li><li>💌 <strong>Bring back the chatty email.</strong> Maybe you'd appreciate the modern-day pen pal. Drop a line!</li><li>✍️ <strong>Remember the follow-up. </strong>If a friend tells you about a doctor's appointment, big interview or family problem, check back in afterward. </li></ul><p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> Friendship can feel like something that should happen naturally. But as life gets busier, a little intentionality becomes essential.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47304098.444010/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cubnl0aW1lcy5jb20vMjAyNi8wOC8yOC93ZWxsL2ZyaWVuZHNoaXAtc29jaWFsLXJpbmdzLmh0bWw_dW5sb2NrZWRfYXJ0aWNsZV9jb2RlPTEuOWxBLlY1cUEuMFJhR0NsVkxVcVphJnNtaWQ9dXJsLXNoYXJlJnV0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aCf8d67b49\" target=\"_blank\"><em>Read the NYT story</em></a><em> </em>(<em>gift link).</em></li></ul></td>\r\n</tr> <tr><td align=\"center\" style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 25px\">   <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47304098.444010/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZmFjZWJvb2suY29tL3NoYXJlci5waHA_dT1odHRwczovL3d3dy5heGlvcy5jb20vbmV3c2xldHRlcnMvYXhpb3MtZmluaXNoLWxpbmUtNDRkN2NiZDktYzE3ZS00NzU1LTgwNGQtN2JkN2Y2NjEyYm